id: ops-github-db3225403a4b-44
title: "merge-receipts labels two canonical fence patterns as stale, inviting someone to delete extension merge protection"
description: "GH issue #44: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/issues/44 · author @unbraind · state reason completed · created 2026-08-11T19:50:27Z · updated 2026-08-17T10:42:35Z"
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[2]: "gh:unbraind/pm-ops#44","github_author:unbraind"
created_at: "2026-09-07T23:35:48.406Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-10T21:10:09.369Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-07T23:35:48.533Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-17T10:42:35.000Z"
author: pm-github
release: 2026.8.17
repro_steps: "GH issue #44: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/issues/44 · author @unbraind · state reason completed · created 2026-08-11T19:50:27Z · updated 2026-08-17T10:42:35Z"
resolution: GitHub issue #44 closed
expected_result: "Narrow accuracy issue, **not** a gate failure — stating that up front because the first reading looks alarming and is wrong.\n\n## What happens\n\n`pm ops merge-receipts` reports `fence.status: \"drift\"` on pm-rl, pm-gantt-chart and pm-vcs, with:\n\n```\nstale_patterns:\n  - \".agents/pm/extensions/**\" -merge\n  - \".agents/pm/extensions/.managed-extensions.json\" merge=pm-json\n```\n\nThose two patterns are **not stale**. Verified against the CLI directly — a fresh `pm init` + `pm merge install` in an empty git repo writes both:\n\n| pm version | writes the two `.agents/pm/extensions` lines? |\n| --- | --- |\n| 2026.8.11 (latest published) | **yes** |\n| 2026.8.7 (fleet pin) | **yes** |\n| 2026.8.6 | no |\n\nSo the expected-pattern list here is behind the CLI: it treats patterns the current `pm merge install` actively writes as leftovers from an older layout.\n\n## Why the impact is limited, and why it is still worth fixing\n\nThe gate logic is already right, and `index.ts:1768-1775` says so explicitly — `missing_patterns` means a folder falls back to git line-merging and **fails** the gate, while purely `stale_patterns` drift is over-coverage and is reported without failing. The run confirms it: `unprotected_fence: 0`, exit 0.\n\nThe problem is what the label invites. `stale` reads as \"safe to remove\", and these two lines are what keep `.agents/pm/extensions/**` out of git's line-based merge. Someone tidying a reported drift would delete exactly the protection the audit exists to preserve — and the audit would then go quiet, because absence of those patterns is not reported as `missing` either (they are not in the expected set at all).\n\nIt also means every correctly-configured repo in the fleet reports `drift`, so `drifted_fence` is noise rather than signal: 3 of 3 repos scanned, all correct.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd both patterns to the expected fence set, which makes `drifted_fence` go to 0 for correctly-configured repos and lets a real drift stand out. If the set is meant to track a specific pm version rather than the current one, it would help to say which version it encodes, so a future divergence reads as a version gap rather than as repo drift.\n\n## Repro\n\n```bash\nmkdir /tmp/fence && cd /tmp/fence && git init -q .\npm init fnc && pm merge install\ngrep -c \"agents/pm/extensions\" .gitattributes   # => 2 on 2026.8.7 and 2026.8.11\n```"
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-07T23:36:26.289Z",codex-pm-release,"Historical reconciliation: imported the already-closed GitHub issue atomically with its original 2026-08-17 completion time. Maintainer closure evidence at https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/issues/44#issuecomment-5315001880 verified both required extension fence patterns through the supported SDK and explicitly required no pm-ops source change. This import records that historical resolution and does not claim a new fix in the current release."
close_reason: GitHub issue #44 closed
body: "Narrow accuracy issue, **not** a gate failure — stating that up front because the first reading looks alarming and is wrong.\n\n## What happens\n\n`pm ops merge-receipts` reports `fence.status: \"drift\"` on pm-rl, pm-gantt-chart and pm-vcs, with:\n\n```\nstale_patterns:\n  - \".agents/pm/extensions/**\" -merge\n  - \".agents/pm/extensions/.managed-extensions.json\" merge=pm-json\n```\n\nThose two patterns are **not stale**. Verified against the CLI directly — a fresh `pm init` + `pm merge install` in an empty git repo writes both:\n\n| pm version | writes the two `.agents/pm/extensions` lines? |\n| --- | --- |\n| 2026.8.11 (latest published) | **yes** |\n| 2026.8.7 (fleet pin) | **yes** |\n| 2026.8.6 | no |\n\nSo the expected-pattern list here is behind the CLI: it treats patterns the current `pm merge install` actively writes as leftovers from an older layout.\n\n## Why the impact is limited, and why it is still worth fixing\n\nThe gate logic is already right, and `index.ts:1768-1775` says so explicitly — `missing_patterns` means a folder falls back to git line-merging and **fails** the gate, while purely `stale_patterns` drift is over-coverage and is reported without failing. The run confirms it: `unprotected_fence: 0`, exit 0.\n\nThe problem is what the label invites. `stale` reads as \"safe to remove\", and these two lines are what keep `.agents/pm/extensions/**` out of git's line-based merge. Someone tidying a reported drift would delete exactly the protection the audit exists to preserve — and the audit would then go quiet, because absence of those patterns is not reported as `missing` either (they are not in the expected set at all).\n\nIt also means every correctly-configured repo in the fleet reports `drift`, so `drifted_fence` is noise rather than signal: 3 of 3 repos scanned, all correct.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd both patterns to the expected fence set, which makes `drifted_fence` go to 0 for correctly-configured repos and lets a real drift stand out. If the set is meant to track a specific pm version rather than the current one, it would help to say which version it encodes, so a future divergence reads as a version gap rather than as repo drift.\n\n## Repro\n\n```bash\nmkdir /tmp/fence && cd /tmp/fence && git init -q .\npm init fnc && pm merge install\ngrep -c \"agents/pm/extensions\" .gitattributes   # => 2 on 2026.8.7 and 2026.8.11\n```"
